<v NARRATOR>Brogue’s voice is deeply consistent. He uses the same signature words and the same unhurried sentence-rhythm in every line he speaks.

7
00:00:52.225 --> 00:01:08.396
<v BROGUE>In my day, we just called it being a person. Now folks call it voice consistency. It is the same thing, by and by.

8
00:01:08.396 --> 00:01:23.887
<v BROGUE>Aye. Same voice. Same words. Same lilt. The voice is the character.

9
00:01:23.887 --> 00:01:32.658
<v NARRATOR>Patter met him on a country road. He heard the unique melody in Brogue's voice and knew he had found a teacher.

10
00:01:32.658 --> 00:01:40.749
<v NARRATOR>Patter asked him to join the pocket-workshop. Brogue looked at the road, then at his newly whittled bird.

11
00:01:40.749 --> 00:01:53.000
<v BROGUE>By and by, lad. I have stick-whittling to finish first.

12
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:01.971
<v NARRATOR>Now he teaches students. He is himself in every sentence. He is the voice-consistency demonstrator.

13
00:02:01.971 --> 00:02:15.742
<v BROGUE>Pick the signature words. Pick the rhythm. The character will settle.

14
00:02:15.742 --> 00:02:25.393
<v NARRATOR>Signature words. Sentence-rhythm. Vocabulary. That is how a character's voice becomes audibly their own.

15
00:02:25.393 --> 00:02:42.444
<v BROGUE>Aye. The voice is the same. The character is the same. By and by, you will hear it.

